= ψ Constitutive Relations =

{{Plain_Hook
| in_one_sentence    = ψ constitutive relations introduce the ψ susceptibility χ_ψ — the single most important new material parameter in Psionic Engineering — and from it derive ψ permittivity, permeability, loss tangent, quality factor, and coherence length as a unified family of measurable, falsifiable material parameters that transform the psionic field equation from a vacuum theory into an engineering discipline applicable to real fabricated structures.
| everyday_analogy   = Maxwell's equations describe electromagnetic fields in vacuum perfectly. But vacuum is not where antennas, capacitors, or waveguides live. To engineer real devices, Maxwell's equations need material equations — D = εE tells you how a dielectric stores electric field; B = μH tells you how a ferrite guides magnetic flux. Without these constitutive relations, Maxwell's equations are a theory of empty space, not a basis for electronics. ψ constitutive relations do the same thing for the psionic field: they connect the abstract field equation to the concrete world of fabricated metamaterial shells, composite fills, and measurable laboratory samples.
| what_it_predicts   = (1) A ψ susceptibility χ_ψ for every material that is in principle measurable — even if currently unknown, the measurement protocol is defined here; (2) A ψ displacement field D_ψ = ε_ψ ψ that modifies how ψ propagates inside a material relative to vacuum; (3) A ψ permeability μ_ψ that governs ψ flux transport and — crucially — sets the effective mass m_eff and therefore the coherence length L_ψ through the dispersion relation from [[ψ Dispersion Relations]] (A3); (4) A null-material baseline defining what it means for a material to be ψ-transparent; (5) A modified wave equation inside any material that shows explicitly how ε_ψ and μ_ψ shift the ψ wave speed and effective mass.
| if_you_remember_one_thing = χ_ψ is to Psionic Engineering what the dielectric constant is to electrical engineering — the single number that tells you how strongly a material interacts with the ψ field. Every other material parameter in this branch is derived from χ_ψ. Every TML-1 measurement is ultimately a measurement of χ_ψ for a specific material at a specific frequency and field condition.
