ψ constitutive relations are the keystone of [[Psionic Engineering]]. Branch A established the field equation, its conservation laws, dispersion properties, boundary conditions, and Green's functions — all in vacuum or in an abstract medium described only by effective mass <math>m_{\rm eff}</math>. Branch B exists to ask: where does <math>m_{\rm eff}</math> come from in a real material? How does a fabricated [[TPMS Lattice Design]] shell filled with [[Smart Composite Fill]] actually modify the ψ field that passes through it?

The answer requires constitutive relations — material equations that specify the response of matter to the ψ field, exactly as D = εE and B = μH specify the response of matter to electromagnetic fields.

This page defines those relations, introduces the canonical Branch B notation, and establishes the master material parameter table that every subsequent page in this branch populates with one additional column.

== The Gap Branch A Leaves Open ==

The master psionic field equation:

:<math>\Box\psi - m^2\psi - \lambda\psi^3 = \alpha F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu} + J_\psi^{\rm (src)}</math>

treats <math>m</math> as a fixed vacuum parameter. But in the [[Psi Stabilizer]] shell, ψ propagates through a composite of BST nanoparticles, MWCNT-loaded epoxy, PZT-5H powder, quartz filler, and ferrofluid. The question that Branch A cannot answer:

* How does this composite store ψ field energy? → requires <math>\varepsilon_\psi</math>
* How easily does ψ flux propagate through it? → requires <math>\mu_\psi</math>
* How much ψ energy is dissipated per cycle? → requires <math>\tan\delta_\psi</math>
* What is the effective mass inside the material? → requires the constitutive relation connecting <math>\mu_\psi</math> to <math>m_{\rm eff}</math>

Constitutive relations are the missing link. They are not derived from the master equation — they are additional physical postulates, informed by the structure of the theory and constrained by experimental measurement. This is exactly the same logical status as Maxwell's constitutive relations: D = εE is not derived from Maxwell's equations; it is an experimental fact about how dielectric materials behave.

== ψ Susceptibility: The Fundamental Parameter ==

=== Definition ===

The ψ susceptibility <math>\chi_\psi</math> characterises how strongly a material polarises in response to an applied ψ field. It is defined by the '''ψ polarisation density''':

:<math>\boxed{P_\psi(\mathbf{x},t) = \varepsilon_0\,\chi_\psi\,\psi(\mathbf{x},t)}</math>

{{Math_Bridge
| equation_label = ψ polarisation density
| equation       = <math>P_\psi = \varepsilon_0\,\chi_\psi\,\psi</math>
| in_english     =
* <math>P_\psi</math> — ψ polarisation density: the material's ψ dipole moment per unit volume induced by the applied field. Units: same as ψ field times ε₀.
* <math>\chi_\psi</math> — ψ susceptibility: a dimensionless number (in the linear, isotropic limit) specifying how strongly the material responds. <math>\chi_\psi = 0</math> — material does not respond (null material). <math>\chi_\psi > 0</math> — material polarises in the same direction as applied ψ (paramagnetic analogue). <math>\chi_\psi < 0</math> — material polarises in opposition (diamagnetic analogue — potentially useful for ψ shielding).
* <math>\varepsilon_0</math> — included to maintain dimensional consistency with the electromagnetic analogy.
* This is the linear, isotropic, instantaneous constitutive relation. Generalisations (nonlinear, anisotropic, dispersive) are introduced below.
}}

=== Physical Meaning ===

<math>\chi_\psi</math> answers the engineering question: "If I apply a ψ field of amplitude <math>\psi_0</math> to this material, how much additional ψ polarisation is induced?" A material with high <math>\chi_\psi</math> amplifies the local ψ field through polarisation back-reaction; a material with <math>\chi_\psi = 0</math> is invisible to ψ.

Three baseline cases establish the scale:

'''Null material''' (<math>\chi_\psi = 0</math>): ψ field passes through unmodified. The material is completely ψ-transparent. Most conventional inorganic, non-biological materials are hypothesised to be null materials or near-null materials until TML-1 measurements demonstrate otherwise.

'''Weakly responding material''' (<math>0 < \chi_\psi \ll 1</math>): Small polarisation, small modification of ψ propagation. The BST composite fill is expected to fall in this regime — its value is a TML-1 measurement target.

'''Strongly responding material''' (<math>\chi_\psi \sim 1</math> or larger): Significant polarisation, major modification of effective mass and wave speed. Biological cortical tissue during high-coherence states is hypothesised (Track B) to approach this regime — it is the highest-priority TML-3 measurement target.

== ψ Permittivity and the Displacement Field ==

=== Definition ===

Define the '''ψ displacement field''':

:<math>\boxed{D_\psi = \varepsilon_0\psi + P_\psi = \varepsilon_0(1 + \chi_\psi)\psi \equiv \varepsilon_\psi\,\psi}</math>

where the '''ψ permittivity''' is:

:<math>\varepsilon_\psi = \varepsilon_0(1 + \chi_\psi)</math>

{{Math_Bridge
| equation_label = ψ displacement field and permittivity
| equation       = <math>D_\psi = \varepsilon_\psi\,\psi,\qquad \varepsilon_\psi = \varepsilon_0(1+\chi_\psi)</math>
| in_english     =
* <math>D_\psi</math> — the ψ displacement field: combines the vacuum ψ field with the material polarisation response. In a null material (<math>\chi_\psi = 0</math>): <math>D_\psi = \varepsilon_0\psi</math> — the free-space result.
* <math>\varepsilon_\psi</math> — the ψ permittivity. Plays the same role as the electric permittivity ε in electromagnetism.
* High <math>\varepsilon_\psi</math>: material stores a large amount of ψ field energy per unit volume per unit field amplitude. Slows ψ wave speed (see modified wave equation below).
* Low <math>\varepsilon_\psi</math>: material stores little ψ energy. ψ passes through rapidly.
* <math>\varepsilon_\psi = \varepsilon_0</math> in vacuum (<math>\chi_\psi = 0</math>). This is always the reference baseline.
}}

=== Complex Permittivity and Loss ===

For a dispersive, lossy material, <math>\varepsilon_\psi</math> is complex and frequency-dependent:

:<math>\varepsilon_\psi(\omega) = \varepsilon'_\psi(\omega) - i\varepsilon''_\psi(\omega)</math>

where:
* <math>\varepsilon'_\psi</math> — real part: ψ energy storage (conservative response)
* <math>\varepsilon''_\psi</math> — imaginary part: ψ energy dissipation (resistive response)

The ratio defines the loss tangent (see [[ψ Loss Tangent]] B4):

:<math>\tan\delta_\psi = \frac{\varepsilon''_\psi}{\varepsilon'_\psi}</math>

The full treatment of <math>\varepsilon_\psi(\omega)</math> and its measurement is in [[ψ Permittivity]] (B2). The loss tangent budget is in [[ψ Loss Tangent]] (B4).

== ψ Permeability and Flux ==

=== Definition ===

The ψ current density <math>\mathbf{J}_\psi = -(\partial_t\psi)\nabla\psi</math> from [[ψ Conservation Laws]] (A2) plays the role of ψ flux. The '''ψ permeability''' <math>\mu_\psi</math> governs how easily this flux propagates through a material:

:<math>\boxed{B_\psi = \mu_\psi\,\mathbf{J}_\psi = \mu_\psi\,(-\partial_t\psi\,\nabla\psi)}</math>

{{Math_Bridge
| equation_label = ψ flux density and permeability
| equation       = <math>B_\psi = \mu_\psi\,\mathbf{J}_\psi</math>
| in_english     =
* <math>B_\psi</math> — ψ flux density: how much ψ current flows per unit cross-sectional area in the material.
* <math>\mu_\psi</math> — ψ permeability: how easily ψ flux passes through. High <math>\mu_\psi</math> means the material guides ψ flux strongly (analogous to iron guiding magnetic flux). Low <math>\mu_\psi</math> means flux passes through with difficulty.
* <math>\mathbf{J}_\psi = -(\partial_t\psi)\nabla\psi</math> — the ψ current density from A2, which equals the ψ Poynting vector <math>\vec{S}_\psi</math>.
* '''Critical connection to m_eff:''' High <math>\mu_\psi</math> increases the effective mass <math>m_{\rm eff}</math>, which decreases the coherence length <math>L_\psi = 1/m_{\rm eff}</math>. The ferrofluid channels in the [[Smart Composite Fill]] (B8) tune <math>\mu_\psi</math> in real time by changing the ferrofluid magnetisation — this is the physical mechanism of coherence length control.
}}

=== The μ_ψ — m_eff Connection ===

The connection between ψ permeability and effective mass is the most important engineering relationship in this page. In a material with permittivity <math>\varepsilon_\psi</math> and permeability <math>\mu_\psi</math>, the wave equation for ψ inside the material becomes (derived in the modified wave equation section below):

:<math>m_{\rm eff}^2(\text{in material}) = m_{\rm eff}^2(\text{vacuum}) + \delta m^2(\mu_\psi)</math>

where <math>\delta m^2(\mu_\psi)</math> is the material-induced mass shift, increasing with <math>\mu_\psi</math>. The explicit form:

:<math>\delta m^2 = \frac{\mu_\psi - \mu_0}{\mu_0}\,m_0^2</math>

where <math>m_0</math> is the vacuum bare mass. Therefore:

:<math>L_\psi(\text{in material}) = \frac{1}{m_{\rm eff}(\mu_\psi)} < L_\psi(\text{vacuum})</math>

High-<math>\mu_\psi</math> material → smaller coherence length. This is the engineering chain:
<center>Ferrofluid H-field → <math>M(H)</math> (Langevin) → <math>\mu_{\rm eff}(H)</math> → <math>m_{\rm eff}</math> → <math>L_\psi</math></center>

The full treatment is in [[ψ Permeability]] (B3) and [[ψ Coherence Length]] (B6).

== Anisotropic Tensor Form ==

For metamaterials with cubic or lower symmetry — including the [[TPMS Lattice Design]] (B7) after the field-cure alignment step from [[Smart Composite Fill]] (B8) — the constitutive relations generalise to tensor form:

:<math>D_\psi^i = \sum_j \varepsilon_\psi^{ij}\,\psi^j, \qquad B_\psi^i = \sum_j \mu_\psi^{ij}\,J_\psi^j</math>

The field-cure alignment step in B8 deliberately introduces uniaxial anisotropy along the z-axis (the ψ₀₁₀ mode polarisation direction from [[ψ Boundary Conditions]] A5):

:<math>[\varepsilon_\psi] = \begin{pmatrix} \varepsilon_\psi^{xx} & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & \varepsilon_\psi^{xx} & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & \varepsilon_\psi^{zz} \end{pmatrix}, \qquad \varepsilon_\psi^{zz} > \varepsilon_\psi^{xx} = \varepsilon_\psi^{yy}</math>

This anisotropy is an engineering feature: the z-axis enhancement increases coupling efficiency to the ψ₀₁₀ mode (which is polarised along z in the oblate spheroidal geometry of the [[Psi Stabilizer]]) without affecting the isotropic mode-selection properties of the TPMS geometry in the transverse plane.

Target after field cure: <math>\varepsilon_\psi^{zz}/\varepsilon_\psi^{xx} > 1.25</math> (from B8 quality control table).

== The Modified Wave Equation in Material ==

Inside a material with constitutive parameters <math>\varepsilon_\psi</math> and <math>\mu_\psi</math>, the ψ field equation becomes:

:<math>\varepsilon_\psi\mu_\psi\,\partial_t^2\psi - \nabla^2\psi - m_{\rm eff}^2\psi - \lambda\psi^3 = \alpha F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu} + J_\psi^{\rm (src)}</math>

Comparing to the vacuum equation <math>(\partial_t^2 - \nabla^2 - m_{\rm eff}^2)\psi = \ldots</math>, the material introduces two modifications:

'''Modification 1 — ψ wave speed reduction:'''

:<math>v_\psi = \frac{1}{\sqrt{\varepsilon_\psi\mu_\psi}} < c</math>

{{Math_Bridge
| equation_label = ψ wave speed in material
| equation       = <math>v_\psi = \frac{1}{\sqrt{\varepsilon_\psi\mu_\psi}}</math>
| in_english     =
* Exactly analogous to the EM result <math>c_{\rm medium} = 1/\sqrt{\varepsilon\mu} = c/n</math>.
* In a material with <math>\varepsilon_\psi = \varepsilon_0</math> and <math>\mu_\psi = \mu_0</math> (null material): <math>v_\psi = 1/\sqrt{\varepsilon_0\mu_0} = c</math> — vacuum speed recovered.
* In the [[TPMS Lattice Design]] composite: <math>\varepsilon_\psi > \varepsilon_0</math> and <math>\mu_\psi > \mu_0</math> (estimated), so <math>v_\psi < c</math>. The ψ wave slows inside the shell.
* '''Engineering implication:''' The resonant frequency of the [[Psi Stabilizer]] cavity is <math>\omega_{010} = \sqrt{m_{\rm eff}^2 + k_{010}^2}</math> where <math>k_{010}</math> depends on <math>v_\psi</math> inside the shell. Specifying the target resonant frequency therefore requires knowing <math>\varepsilon_\psi\mu_\psi</math> for the composite — the constitutive parameters are not optional inputs.
}}

'''Modification 2 — effective mass shift:'''

The material-modified dispersion relation (from [[ψ Dispersion Relations]] A3, extended to material):

:<math>\omega^2 = v_\psi^2\,k^2 + m_{\rm eff,mat}^2</math>

where:

:<math>m_{\rm eff,mat}^2 = \varepsilon_\psi\mu_\psi\,m_{\rm eff,vac}^2</math>

High <math>\varepsilon_\psi\mu_\psi</math> product increases the effective mass inside the material — which shortens the coherence length. This is a second mechanism (in addition to the direct <math>\mu_\psi</math>-to-<math>m_{\rm eff}</math> connection above) by which the composite fill controls <math>L_\psi</math>.

== The Null Material Baseline ==

'''Definition:''' A material is a '''ψ null material''' if <math>\chi_\psi = 0</math>, equivalently <math>\varepsilon_\psi = \varepsilon_0</math> and <math>\mu_\psi = \mu_0</math>.

A null material:
* Does not store ψ energy (<math>P_\psi = 0</math>)
* Does not modify ψ wave speed (<math>v_\psi = c</math>)
* Does not modify ψ effective mass (<math>m_{\rm eff,mat} = m_{\rm eff,vac}</math>)
* Is completely ψ-transparent

'''Working assumption:''' All conventional inorganic, non-biological, non-engineered materials are null materials until TML-1 measurements demonstrate otherwise. This is the conservative baseline — consistent with the absence of documented ψ-material interactions in the existing scientific literature (see [[Scientific Foundations of Psionics]]).

The assumption is falsifiable: if VNA S-parameter measurements on a control sample (air-gap fixture, no material) and a material-filled fixture show no difference in the ψ-proxy measurable (e.g., resonant frequency of the ψ cavity does not shift when material is introduced), the null material hypothesis is confirmed for that material.

This is a Track A measurement — it requires no assumption that ψ is real, only that the device's resonant frequency is sensitive to the material in its cavity through the modified wave equation above.

== Frequency Dispersion ==

Like all material response functions, <math>\varepsilon_\psi(\omega)</math> and <math>\mu_\psi(\omega)</math> are frequency-dependent in general. The Kramers–Kronig relations constrain this dispersion:

:<math>\varepsilon'_\psi(\omega) - \varepsilon_0 = \frac{2}{\pi}\,\mathcal{P}\!\int_0^\infty \frac{\omega'\,\varepsilon''_\psi(\omega')}{\omega'^2 - \omega^2}\,d\omega'</math>

This is not merely a mathematical identity — it is a causality constraint. A material cannot have a loss peak (large <math>\varepsilon''_\psi</math> at some frequency) without a corresponding dispersion feature in <math>\varepsilon'_\psi</math> nearby. The Kramers–Kronig relations therefore provide a self-consistency check on any claimed set of ψ material parameters: if a measurement gives <math>\varepsilon''_\psi(\omega)</math> but the corresponding <math>\varepsilon'_\psi(\omega)</math> does not satisfy the Kramers–Kronig integral, the measurement is inconsistent and should be repeated.

